WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.863
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:08.711
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:16.377
Unit twenty-eight: The Circular Economy. Today you will describe change using replacing X with Y structures.

4
00:00:16.677 --> 00:00:19.411
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.634
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.856
Circular economy.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.810
Circular economy means an economic model that keeps materials in use as long as possible.

8
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:35.531
A circular economy designs waste out of the system.

9
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:39.303
Your turn. Say: Circular economy.

10
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:43.946
Circular economy.

11
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:45.615
Reuse.

12
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:50.439
Reuse means using an item again for the same or another purpose.

13
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.758
Reuse of containers cut packaging costs.

14
00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.608
Your turn. Say: Reuse.

15
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:00.630
Reuse.

16
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:02.431
Recycling.

17
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:06.415
Recycling means turning waste into new materials.

18
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:10.119
Recycling saves energy compared with new production.

19
00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:13.520
Your turn. Say: Recycling.

20
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:17.530
Recycling.

21
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:19.110
Waste.

22
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:23.208
Waste means material that is discarded as useless.

23
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:27.151
Waste is a design failure, not an inevitability.

24
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.402
Your turn. Say: Waste.

25
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:34.268
Waste.

26
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:35.855
Design.

27
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.278
Design means the planning of how a product is made and used.

28
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.813
Good design makes repair easy.

29
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.651
Your turn. Say: Design.

30
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.292
Design.

31
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:51.074
Resources.

32
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:56.033
Resources means materials, energy and labour available for use.

33
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.288
Finite resources require careful use.

34
00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:02.594
Your turn. Say: Resources.

35
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:06.713
Resources.

36
00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:08.962
Product lifecycle.

37
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.830
Product lifecycle means the stages of a product from design to disposal.

38
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:18.645
A product lifecycle includes manufacture, use and recovery.

39
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.285
Your turn. Say: Product lifecycle.

40
00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:26.715
Product lifecycle.

41
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.254
Linear.

42
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.039
Linear means following a take-make-dispose path.

43
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.404
A linear economy turns resources into waste.

44
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:38.681
Your turn. Say: Linear.

45
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:42.383
Linear.

46
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.665
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:52.201
Visal, my phone broke after two years and I just threw it away. Is that normal?

48
00:02:53.317 --> 00:03:00.717
In a linear economy, yes: take, make, dispose. The circular model replaces waste with reuse.

49
00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:03.073
How would that look?

50
00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:13.973
Designers replace single-use packaging with refillable containers, and manufacturers replace glued parts with repairable ones.

51
00:03:15.083 --> 00:03:16.514
Does it actually work?

52
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:28.930
It can. Firms that replace disposal with take-back schemes recover valuable materials, and cities that replace landfill with recycling save money.

53
00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:31.695
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.711
Grammar focus: Replacing X with Y structures.

55
00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:37.302
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.156
The model replaces waste with reuse.

57
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:45.070
Designers replace single-use packaging with refillable containers.

58
00:03:45.983 --> 00:03:48.819
Cities replace landfill with recycling to save money.

59
00:03:49.767 --> 00:04:00.665
replace X with Y (not 'replace X to Y'): X is removed, Y is introduced. Passive: Waste is replaced with reuse. Alternative: substitute Y for X.

60
00:04:01.417 --> 00:04:05.369
Be careful! Don't say: The model replaces waste to reuse.

61
00:04:07.333 --> 00:04:09.913
Say: The model replaces waste with reuse.

62
00:04:10.213 --> 00:04:11.958
replace X with Y.

63
00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:15.198
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.117
Closing the Loop.

65
00:04:17.417 --> 00:04:24.293
A linear economy takes resources, makes products and disposes of them, which wastes materials and creates pollution.

66
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:32.257
The circular economy aims to keep materials in use by design, so the model replaces waste with reuse, repair and recycling.

67
00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:41.164
Designers replace single-use packaging with refillable containers, and manufacturers replace glued components with parts that can be repaired.

68
00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:47.371
Across the product lifecycle, firms recover valuable resources instead of buying new ones.

69
00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:53.420
Critics note that recycling alone is not enough, so reducing consumption remains essential.

70
00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:58.570
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:59.417 --> 00:05:01.892
The model replaces waste blank reuse.

72
00:05:02.192 --> 00:05:05.486
A, with. B, to. C, by of.

73
00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:10.499
The answer is A: with.

74
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.147
An economy that follows take-make-dispose is blank.

75
00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:19.170
A, circular. B, linear. C, recycled.

76
00:05:22.733 --> 00:05:24.826
The answer is B: linear.

77
00:05:25.983 --> 00:05:27.878
Using an item again is blank.

78
00:05:28.178 --> 00:05:31.925
A, waste. B, design. C, reuse.

79
00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.431
The answer is C: reuse.

80
00:05:38.583 --> 00:05:40.293
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:40.593 --> 00:05:47.984
A, They replace plastic with glass.. B, They replace plastic to glass.. C, They replace with plastic glass..

82
00:05:51.533 --> 00:05:54.555
The answer is A: They replace plastic with glass..

83
00:05:55.717 --> 00:05:59.829
According to the reading, recycling alone is not enough, so reducing…

84
00:06:00.129 --> 00:06:05.629
A, consumption remains essential. B, design is useless. C, reuse is wrong.

85
00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:12.408
The answer is A: consumption remains essential.

86
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.740
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.583 --> 00:06:21.057
How could your community adopt circular economy ideas?

88
00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.385
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.685 --> 00:07:02.460
Our community could replace single-use plastic bags with reusable ones, set up repair workshops and organise a collection point for old electronics and clothes. Markets could accept refillable containers, and compost could replace burning of organic waste. Small steps like these keep materials in use and save money.

90
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.411
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:05.317 --> 00:07:09.850
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and replacing x with y structures.

92
00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.706
Today's value is resourcefulness. Make more from less and waste nothing.

93
00:07:15.006 --> 00:07:17.775
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:18.075 --> 00:07:21.342
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

